:: day0_npc_marta3 [nobr]
<<npcPortrait "marta" "Marta">>
<h3>Marta — The Last Pastry</h3>

<p>She's been baking since midnight. The evidence is everywhere — every surface covered, every rack loaded, the output of a woman who processes anxiety through butter and flour. The kitchen staff move around her the way water moves around a boulder — with respect and without questioning the boulder's right to be there.</p>

<p>She sees me in the doorway and stops. Sets down the rolling pin. Wipes her hands on her apron. She doesn't speak for a moment.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">MARTA</span>"Sit," she says. Her voice is different. Not softer — Marta doesn't do soft. But the usual declarative force is tempered by something. She points to the stool. My stool. The permanent one.</div>

<p>I sit. She puts a plate in front of me. One pastry. Not cheese — apple, this time. Cinnamon and sugar and thin-sliced fruit in a latticed crust. She hasn't made apple pastries since I was twelve.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">MARTA</span>"I made your favourite," she says. "The one you used to beg for when you were little. Before you got too princely to beg."</div>

<p>I look at the pastry. I look at Marta. Her eyes are red. She's been crying. Marta — who I have never, in twenty years, seen show anything other than volcanic confidence and wooden-spoon authority — has been crying in her kitchen at midnight while baking pastries from my childhood.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Marta—"</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">MARTA</span>"Eat it. Don't talk. Just eat it."</div>

<p>I eat it. The flavour is a time machine — twelve years old, sitting on this stool (it was taller then, or I was shorter), legs swinging, watching Marta roll pastry and telling her about my day in the breathless, overlapping sentences of a child who hasn't yet learned that adults don't always listen. She always listened. She listened the way she cooked — with full attention, with care, with the understanding that the thing being made deserved respect.</p>

<p>The pastry is perfect. The cinnamon is warm. The apple is sharp. The sugar is sweet.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">MARTA</span>"Whatever happens today," Marta says, her back to me, stirring the pot that doesn't need stirring, "whatever changes — you come to my kitchen. You hear me? You come and sit on that stool and I will feed you. I don't care what you look like. I don't care what name you carry. You sit on that stool and you are my boy. Always."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"I might not be a boy anymore, Marta."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">MARTA</span>"Then you'll be my girl. Same stool. Same pastries. Same Marta."</div>

<p>I stand. I walk around the prep table. I hug her. She's solid — barrel-built, flour-dusted, smelling of butter and yeast and the particular warmth of a woman who has fed a palace for thirty years. She hugs me back. Hard. The wooden spoon presses against my shoulder blade. She's still holding it. Of course she is.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">MARTA</span>"Go," she says, releasing me, turning away so I can't see her face. "Go do what you need to do. And come back hungry."</div>

<<set $d0_npc_marta3 to true>>
<<set $energy += 10>>
<<set $stress -= 10>>
<<set $repStaff += 2>>

<div class="scene-end">
  [[Continue → |day0_kitchen]]
</div>
